The tests confirm the bug. The `processJob` method returns `success: true` even when the handler throws an error. Looking at the code in `src/queue.ts`:

```typescript
async processJob(
  job: Job<T>,
  handler: (payload: T) => Promise<void> | void
): Promise<{ success: boolean; job: Job<T> }> {
  const current: Job<T> = { ...job, attempts: job.attempts + 1 };
  try {
    await handler(current.payload);
    return { success: true, job: current };
  } catch (err) {
    // BUG: swallows error and reports success
    return { success: true, job: current };
  }
}
```

The catch block returns `success: true` instead of `success: false`. Also, the tests expect the job to be re-enqueued when it fails (for retry), but the current code doesn't do that either.

Looking at the test:
```typescript
test("processJob retry re-enqueues", async () => {
    const q = new AsyncQueue<string>({ retryDelay: 0 });
    const job = q.enqueue("fail", { maxAttempts: 3 });
